        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Species Sub-page · SPC·08d</p>
    <h1>Forest Raven Diet: What This Species Eats</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Forest Raven (<em>Corvus tasmanicus</em>) is an opportunistic omnivore. Carrion, invertebrates, small vertebrates, grain, fruit, and human food waste all feature in its diet. The species exploits both native forest and agricultural and urban habitats, particularly in Tasmania where it is the dominant large corvid. Its diet is broadly similar to that of other large Australian corvids, with some adaptation to the cooler, forested conditions of its core range.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Primary Food Items</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Based on HANZAB species account and Tasmanian corvid field studies:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Carrion:</strong> roadkill (wallabies, wombats, possums, introduced mammals), dead livestock (sheep, cattle). Carrion is a major and reliable food source, particularly along roadsides in Tasmania.</li>
      <li><strong>Invertebrates:</strong> beetles, caterpillars, earthworms, grasshoppers, and other invertebrates; ground-foraging for invertebrates is a primary foraging mode in both forest and agricultural settings.</li>
      <li><strong>Small vertebrates:</strong> skinks, mice, small birds, nestlings — taken opportunistically.</li>
      <li><strong>Eggs:</strong> eggs of ground-nesting birds including quail and plovers.</li>
      <li><strong>Grain and seeds:</strong> cereal grains in agricultural areas; also native seeds.</li>
      <li><strong>Fruit:</strong> various fruits and berries in native forest and orchard settings.</li>
      <li><strong>Human food waste:</strong> rubbish bins, campsites, urban scraps — the species is bold and persistent around human food sources.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Forest and Agricultural Foraging</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Forest Raven's diet shifts with habitat. In native forest, invertebrates, fruit, and eggs are more prominent. On farmland, grain and carrion become more important. Following farm machinery to take exposed earthworms and insects is documented behaviour. In urban Hobart and other Tasmanian towns, the species exploits food waste and compost. The species is highly behaviourally flexible — a major factor in its success across the modified landscapes of Tasmania.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Pages</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/species/forest-raven/">Forest Raven — species hub</a></li>
      <li><a href="/compare/forest-raven-vs-australian-raven/">Forest Raven vs Australian Raven</a></li>
      <li><a href="/questions/what-do-ravens-eat/">What do ravens eat?</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-faq qa-section" aria-labelledby="faq-h2">
